  "intro": [
    {
      "text": "Fly agaric is recognised by a scarlet to orange cap, often dotted with white veil remnants, and pale gills.",
      "state": "KNOWN",
      "source": {
        "label": "Woodland Trust — Fly agaric",
        "url": "https://www.woodlandtrust.org.uk/trees-woods-and-wildlife/fungi-and-lichens/fly-agaric/"
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "It lives with tree roots in mycorrhizal associations, often with birch and also with conifers, helping transport nutrients.",
      "state": "KNOWN",
      "source": {
        "label": "Woodland Trust — Fly agaric",
        "url": "https://www.woodlandtrust.org.uk/trees-woods-and-wildlife/fungi-and-lichens/fly-agaric/"
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "The conspicuous mushroom appears from late summer into early winter in British woodland and heathland. It is poisonous and should not be eaten.",
